# Copy this file to .env in this folder and fill it in, or run pnpm configure at the
# repository root, which asks and writes it (mode 600). .env is git-ignored; never commit a real key.
# The server reads it when ENV_FILE=<path> names it (pnpm start does that). The regression run, the text
# CLI and the cassette trim read it themselves when they reach a model or its cassette (--client record,
# recorded or jev): ENV_FILE=<path> names another file instead, and a variable already in the environment
# wins over the file. There is no need to source it first.
# Nothing here is needed to run the tests, the stub regression or the text CLI.

# Your TypeSafe API key, used by the live and recording clients (--client jev, --client record,
# and the server with JEV_CLIENT=jev). Recording the cassette needs it; replaying one does not.
TYPESAFE_API_KEY=

# Where the decision model is reached: JEV_PROVIDER, default typesafe. Each provider reads its own key.
#   typesafe     TYPESAFE_API_KEY      https://api.typesafe.ai                 model jev-1.13.0
#   openrouter   OPENROUTER_API_KEY    https://openrouter.ai/api               model typesafe/jev-1.13
#   vercel       AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY    https://ai-gateway.vercel.sh/typesafe   model typesafe-ai/jev
#   custom       JEV_API_KEY (optional), JEV_BASE_URL and JEV_MODEL (required): a compatible endpoint,
#                such as an open-weight model that speaks Jev's API. Its probabilities are not Jev's,
#                so the confidence thresholds may need re-measuring: record a cassette with it and
#                compare with regress.
# JEV_BASE_URL and JEV_MODEL also override an official provider's. A base URL must be https unless
# it is localhost. Each model records and replays its own cassette, named after the model.
# JEV_PROVIDER=typesafe
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
# AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY=
# JEV_BASE_URL=
# JEV_MODEL=
# JEV_API_KEY=

# The phone line (pnpm serve) needs these three. A 555 number is fine for local use.
# pnpm start sets PUBLIC_HOST itself when it is unset: it opens a quick tunnel (cloudflared; no
# account) and uses its hostname, which changes every run. --tunnel none runs the server alone.
# PUBLIC_HOST=
# TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=
# HANDOFF_NUMBER=

# Transfer targets (app.yaml's transfer: a handoff to the billing line, a priority queue): each target
# names the variable that holds it, set here like HANDOFF_NUMBER, to an E.164 number or a sip: URI
# (sip:desk@pbx.example.com, which also carries the SIP headers the app sends). The server will not start
# with one unset; pnpm diagnose checks each. Without transfer, every handoff dials HANDOFF_NUMBER.
# This app sends every handoff (urgent, a person asked for, a caller the line could not help) to the
# studio's main line (pnpm configure asks for it). A 555 number is fine for local use; put the
# studio's real line here when it answers real calls. It must ring a person: never the number that
# forwards to this desk when no one picks up, or an urgent caller is put straight back to the desk.
STUDIO_MAIN_LINE=+15555550100

# The carriers the phone line answers, a comma list: twilio (the default), telnyx, or both. Each is
# served on /voice/<carrier>; the plain /voice is Twilio's. A carrier's secret is needed only when it
# is listed: TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN for twilio, and for telnyx the account's base64 Ed25519 public key.
# VOICE_PROVIDERS=twilio
# TELNYX_PUBLIC_KEY=

# The voice each carrier speaks the prompts in, optional (unset, the carrier's default voice). Carriers
# name voices differently, so each has its own: TTS_PROVIDER and TTS_VOICE (set both) are Twilio's,
# e.g. Google and en-US-Neural2-F; TELNYX_VOICE is Telnyx's, a name that carries its engine, e.g.
# Telnyx.NaturalHD.astra.
# TTS_PROVIDER=
# TTS_VOICE=
# TELNYX_VOICE=

# The speech recognizer each carrier hears callers with, for the app's default locale (an app's
# voice.locales.<tag>.recognition wins; other locales get the carrier's default). Twilio's provider
# (Deepgram or Google) and model; unset, Deepgram flux, which is for English: a deployment whose
# app speaks another language by default should name a model that hears it (nova-3-general, say).
# Telnyx's provider; unset, Telnyx's own default.
# TWILIO_TRANSCRIPTION_PROVIDER=Deepgram
# TWILIO_SPEECH_MODEL=flux
# TELNYX_TRANSCRIPTION_PROVIDER=

# Telnyx's event streams on the socket, optional (unset, none): speaker-events (when the agent and the
# caller start and stop speaking) and tokens-played (each line as Telnyx reports it played), space-separated.
# Each is written to the call's frame log; with them, a line Telnyx cuts short can be found (below).
# Quoted so a shell can `source` the file too; the server's own loader takes it with or without quotes.
# TELNYX_EVENTS="speaker-events tokens-played"

# A line the carrier cut short is said again, once: on by default, and active only on a carrier that
# reports its playback (Telnyx with TELNYX_EVENTS). A turn's lines reported finished in under
# RESAY_MIN_FRACTION of their estimated length (0.05 to 0.95, default 0.35), with no interrupt and no
# caller heard, are sent again, so the caller is not left in silence until the no-input wait.
# RESAY_CUT_LINES=on
# RESAY_MIN_FRACTION=0.35

# A turn that ends the call (a goodbye, a transfer's line) holds its end until its lines have played:
# auto (the default) on a carrier that drops what it has not said when the end comes (Telnyx), on for
# every carrier, off to send the end with the lines. The end goes when the carrier reports the lines
# played (Telnyx with TELNYX_EVENTS), else after their estimated length; never later than
# END_PLAYBACK_MAX_MS (default 15000). With the carrier's report, the estimate is only an outer limit for a
# report that never comes: END_PLAYBACK_FACTOR (1 to 10, default 2) times it, plus END_PLAYBACK_MARGIN_MS
# (0 to 60000, default 2000), so a voice slower than the estimate is not cut off.
# END_AFTER_PLAYBACK=auto
# END_PLAYBACK_MAX_MS=15000
# END_PLAYBACK_FACTOR=2
# END_PLAYBACK_MARGIN_MS=2000

# The no-input wait: after a question has played (by its estimated length), NO_INPUT_MS of silence
# (default 7000; 0 turns it off) runs a silence turn, "I didn't hear anything." and the question again.
# On a carrier that reports the caller speaking (Telnyx with TELNYX_EVENTS speaker-events; Twilio's
# partial transcripts end the wait instead), the wait is held while the caller speaks, and runs at least
# NO_INPUT_AFTER_SPEECH_MS (default 2500) after they stop, so their transcript arrives first.
# NO_INPUT_MS=7000
# NO_INPUT_AFTER_SPEECH_MS=2500

# Keys at an offer that takes a yes or a no only (a slot's callerNumber answers: yes-no, offerAnswers:
# yes-no, or app.yaml's textConsent question): each key is held, and the keys are taken as one answer
# KEY_WAIT_MS (default 2000, at least 300) after the last of them, or at once on #. A 1 alone is yes,
# a 2 alone no.
# KEY_WAIT_MS=2000

# A caller who had not finished, on a carrier that reports the caller speaking (Telnyx with TELNYX_EVENTS
# speaker-events), with no interrupt from the carrier: a caller who speaks again no later than
# RESUME_AFTER_PAUSE_MS (default 2000, up to 10000) after they stopped, and no later than
# RESUME_INTO_REPLY_MS (default 1000, up to 5000) after the reply to that prompt went out, is still
# talking: their next prompt continues the one before it (the app's voice.continueWithinMs, 0, turns it off).
# RESUME_AFTER_PAUSE_MS=2000
# RESUME_INTO_REPLY_MS=1000

# On the same carriers (Telnyx with TELNYX_EVENTS speaker-events), an interrupt with no caller heard
# speaking around it is the carrier's, not the caller's: no caller heard within SPURIOUS_INTERRUPT_WINDOW_MS
# (default 700, up to 5000) before it, nor within 0.4 s after it (2 s before the caller has been heard at
# all on the call, as over the greeting). It is not taken as a barge-in, and the lines it cut are said
# again from the start, once. On by default; off takes every interrupt as before.
# RESAY_SPURIOUS_INTERRUPTS=on
# SPURIOUS_INTERRUPT_WINDOW_MS=700

# On the same carriers, the reply to words the model reads as unfinished (its utteranceComplete under
# INCOMPLETE_WAIT_BELOW, 0.05 to 0.95; unset, the gates' GATE_COMPLETE) is held up to INCOMPLETE_WAIT_MS
# (default 1000, up to 3000; 0 turns it off). A caller heard going on in that time is not talked over:
# the reply is never said, and their next prompt continues the one before it. Only a reply that just
# speaks is held (never one after the gate, a service, or the end of the call).
# INCOMPLETE_WAIT_MS=1000
# INCOMPLETE_WAIT_BELOW=0.6

# Who may talk over a line the agent is saying, optional: any (the default: speech or a keypress), speech,
# dtmf (a keypress only) or none. It sets the relay element's interruptible on every carrier. A carrier's
# barge-in can stop the agent's speech on noise or echo; dtmf or none is also a way to rule barge-in in
# or out when callers report not hearing replies. With dtmf or none the lines say they are not interruptible too.
# BARGE_IN_TWILIO and BARGE_IN_TELNYX, each optional with the same values, override BARGE_IN for that
# carrier's calls alone; unset, BARGE_IN applies to it.
# BARGE_IN=any
# BARGE_IN_TWILIO=any
# BARGE_IN_TELNYX=any

# The engine's web chat (a WebSocket on /chat), off by default. On, it needs the sites whose pages may
# open a chat: exact origins, comma-separated (scheme, host and any port, no path), or * on a laptop
# (PUBLIC_HOST=localhost) only. A chat nobody writes to ends after CHAT_IDLE_MS (default 30 minutes);
# until then a dropped connection can resume it. At most CHAT_MAX_SESSIONS chats are live at once
# (default 1000); over it a new chat is told busy, and a resume is still taken. A limit per visitor
# is the reverse proxy's to keep.
# CHAT=off
# CHAT_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://www.example.com
# CHAT_IDLE_MS=1800000
# CHAT_MAX_SESSIONS=1000

# How a chat user signs in: none (the default), jwt, or mock. jwt takes a signed token from the site's
# identity provider (an OpenID Connect ID token, say), verified against its published keys at
# CHAT_JWKS_URL (https only), with CHAT_ISSUER as its iss and CHAT_AUDIENCE in its aud; RS256, ES256
# and EdDSA only. The token claim sub names the subject (identity.yaml's signIn.claim can name another
# claim of the token). mock takes mock:<id>, unsigned, and only when PUBLIC_HOST is localhost.
# CHAT_SIGNIN=none
# CHAT_JWKS_URL=https://id.example.com/.well-known/jwks.json
# CHAT_ISSUER=https://id.example.com
# CHAT_AUDIENCE=

# The web chat widget's script (@dialogwright/widget), served by this server on /widget.js: off by
# default. It is for a laptop or a simple deployment; a production site loads the script from its own
# CDN. When on, /widget.js is public (a site's pages load it), through a tunnel too. WIDGET_FILE is
# the built script; unset, node_modules/@dialogwright/widget/dist/dialogwright-widget.js under this app
# (add @dialogwright/widget to its dependencies), or name the built file. Build it first with
# pnpm --filter @dialogwright/widget build.
# WIDGET=off
# WIDGET_FILE=node_modules/@dialogwright/widget/dist/dialogwright-widget.js

# Stopping (SIGTERM from a service manager, or Ctrl-C): the server stops taking new calls (a new call
# is put through to HANDOFF_NUMBER, a new chat is told busy, and /ready answers 503 while /health
# stays 200) and waits up to DRAIN_MS for its live calls and chats to end, then closes what is left
# (a call still going is put through to HANDOFF_NUMBER); 0 closes at once. A second signal stops at once.
# DRAIN_MS=30000
# DRAIN_UNREADY_MS (0 to 600000; default 0, off): first, before the drain, /ready answers 503 for this
# long while new calls are still answered as usual, so a load balancer sees the server go before a new
# call it still sends would be put through to HANDOFF_NUMBER. With a balancer, set it to at least the
# time the balancer takes to see a 503 (its check interval times the failures it needs); pnpm service
# adds it to the stop timeout.
# DRAIN_UNREADY_MS=0

# Where calls, chats and relay tokens are kept between turns: memory (the default: a restart ends the
# calls under way, as it always has) or file:<dir>, a folder on this machine (relative to where the
# server runs; made mode 700, each file 600, a token only as its hash), which an update, a reboot or a
# crash leaves them in. The restarted server takes the carrier's callback for a call, and the caller
# hears "Sorry, I lost you for a moment." (or the app's own resumed prompt) and the question they were
# asked; a chat resumes with its resume token. With file:, a drain past DRAIN_MS sends no one to a person:
# it hands each live call over to the server that starts next. The carrier's callback is told to wait
# RESTART_PAUSE_S seconds (the caller hears silence) and connect again, by when this server has stopped
# and the restarted one takes the call; set it a little above how long a restart takes; 0 stops
# listening at once instead. After a crash, or a restart slower than the pause, a carrier that calls back
# while no server listens uses its fallback document (pnpm fallback writes one), which puts the caller
# through to HANDOFF_NUMBER: set it in the carrier's console. Saves are not flushed to the disk (a crash
# loses nothing, a power cut the last few seconds) unless SESSION_FSYNC=on. pnpm diagnose checks the folder.
# SESSION_STORE=file:sessions
# RESTART_PAUSE_S=5
# SESSION_FSYNC=off

# Many servers on one database: SESSION_STORE=postgres://<user>:<password>@<host>:5432/<database> (or
# postgresql://) keeps the calls, chats and relay tokens in Postgres, so a call goes on on whichever
# server the carrier reconnects to when the one running it dies or drains. It needs the pg package
# (pnpm add pg where it is not installed). The first start makes the tables (dw_*, with their version),
# which needs a user that may create tables; later starts only read and write them. A database whose
# tables are at another version is refused. One server runs a call's turns at a time: it holds the call
# for CALL_LEASE_MS (500 to 60000), renewed every third of it and timed by the database's clock; a crash
# is taken over at once on the carrier's reconnect. Every question to the database has a deadline,
# SESSION_STORE_TIMEOUT_MS (100 to 30000): past it, a caller is put through to a person. SERVER_ID names
# this server in the log and the lease table (a random suffix is added per process). The connection
# string is never logged: its password is masked. With several servers behind a balancer,
# RESTART_PAUSE_S=0 hands a drained call straight to another server; keep DRAIN_MS longer than the
# balancer takes to see /ready say 503.
# SESSION_STORE=postgres://dialogwright:<password>@db.example:5432/dialogwright
# CALL_LEASE_MS=5000
# SESSION_STORE_TIMEOUT_MS=2000
# SERVER_ID=

# The audit and the traces with several servers. AUDIT_STORE=postgres://... keeps the audit in Postgres
# (it may be the database SESSION_STORE names): a hash chain per call, each call's newest entry anchored
# into a chain for the day, so servers appending at once never fork a chain; pnpm audit:verify takes the
# URL and checks every chain. Unset, the audit is day files in AUDIT_DIR. TRACE_STORE=s3://<bucket>/<prefix>
# writes each call's trace as segments on S3-compatible storage (AWS S3, Cloudflare R2, MinIO and others),
# which the console, replay and kb:gaps join whichever servers wrote them: S3_ENDPOINT (unset: AWS's for
# S3_REGION), S3_REGION (default us-east-1; R2 takes auto), S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID and S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY
# (never logged), and S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=on for a store reached by path (MinIO). A trace write that fails
# never stops a call: it is logged, counted on /health and tried again. Unset, traces are files in
# TRACE_DIR. With SESSION_STORE=postgres the console sees every server's calls, and a chat resumed on
# another server closes its socket on the first. pnpm diagnose checks the database and the bucket.
# AUDIT_STORE=postgres://dialogwright:<password>@db.example:5432/dialogwright
# TRACE_STORE=s3://<bucket>/traces
# S3_ENDPOINT=https://<account>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
# S3_REGION=us-east-1
# S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=off

# The resumed line ("Sorry, I lost you for a moment.") for a call its server let go cleanly (a drain, a
# planned restart) is said only when its caller was left in silence longer than RESUMED_AFTER_MS (0 to
# 60000; 0 says it whenever it was said before): from the carrier's callback to the new socket's setup.
# A crash always says it. A planned restart's pause (RESTART_PAUSE_S, default 5 s) is longer, so it is
# said; a RESTART_PAUSE_S under it lets a quick restart pass unnoticed. The one default that changes
# one machine: a planned restart under 1.5 s of silence gets no apology.
# RESUMED_AFTER_MS=1500

# Pinging relay sockets: every RELAY_PING_MS (0, the default, is off; else 1000 to 120000) the server
# pings each one and closes one that has not answered the last ping, so a socket the carrier abandoned
# without closing it is let go. Turn it on once a test call shows your carrier answers pings.
# RELAY_PING_MS=0

# Keeping the disk in check (the retention policy): unset, every trace and audit day is kept, as the
# server always has (pnpm configure writes the values shown into a new settings file). Traces and their
# frame logs last written more than TRACE_RETENTION_DAYS days ago are deleted, and past TRACE_MAX_GB the
# calls written least recently go first, whole, never a live one (on the trace store too). Audit day
# files more than AUDIT_RETENTION_DAYS days old go (never today's), never by size: deleting audit days
# ends the record for those days, so keep them as long as you must keep the record. The server sweeps at
# startup and once an hour, and /health then reports disk (traceBytes, auditBytes); with
# DISK_WARN_FREE_GB, freeBytes and low too, and the log warns while it is low. Archive traces before they
# go if pnpm kb:gaps should still read them. A trace that cannot be written is logged and counted
# (/health's traces.failures) and never fails a turn; an audit that cannot be written still does.
# TRACE_RETENTION_DAYS=30
# TRACE_MAX_GB=5
# DISK_WARN_FREE_GB=2
# AUDIT_RETENTION_DAYS=

# What each carrier charges a minute, in dollars, for the console's Insights page: the carrier's cost
# of the calls it carried, each call's length rounded up to the minute as carriers bill, beside the
# decision model's cost (which the traces hold exactly). It is the automated part of the inbound call
# only: the time with a person after a transfer, and the transfer's dialed leg, are not in it. Unset,
# Insights shows no carrier cost at all.
# Your rate is on the carrier's pricing page or your invoice; the server refuses to start on one that
# is not a number of dollars from 0 to 100.
# CARRIER_RATE_PER_MIN_TWILIO=0.014
# CARRIER_RATE_PER_MIN_TELNYX=0.007

# The console's daily figures (counts and durations by the hour, no caller's data) are kept in a folder
# .figures in the trace folder (or <prefix>.figures/ on the trace store), and outlive the traces: the
# traces' retention leaves them, so Insights keeps its past. Unset, they are kept (one small file per day);
# set a number of days, no shorter than TRACE_RETENTION_DAYS, to remove older ones.
# FIGURES_RETENTION_DAYS=

# The server's own log: with LOG_FILE, everything it prints is also appended to that file, rolled past
# LOG_MAX_MB (default 10) to <file>.1, <file>.2, ..., keeping LOG_KEEP (default 5). pnpm service sets
# LOG_FILE for launchd on a Mac; systemd keeps the journal. Unset, the output goes where it always went.
# LOG_FILE=
# LOG_MAX_MB=10
# LOG_KEEP=5

# Who may open the operator console (/dashboard). local, the default, answers only a request made on
# this machine, and 404 through the tunnel. token serves it through the tunnel too, behind a sign-in:
# the server writes a one-time link (valid 10 minutes, used once) to CONSOLE_LINK_FILE (mode 600;
# unset, .console-link/link.json beside the trace folder), and pnpm console:link prints a new one.
# Opening it signs one browser in for CONSOLE_SESSION_HOURS (default 12, at most 168) with a signed
# cookie. CONSOLE_SESSION_KEY (32 random bytes or more, hex or base64: openssl rand -hex 32) keeps
# sign-ins, and sign-outs (kept beside the link file), across a restart; unset, a restart signs
# everyone out. Every sign-in, refusal, the live feed's first open and each replay of a stored call
# are appended to the audit as console_access. CONSOLE_CLIENT_ADDRESS is where a request's address
# (for the limit on failed sign-ins per address, and the audit) is read: auto, the default, takes
# cf-connecting-ip, else the last x-forwarded-for entry; cf-connecting-ip for a Cloudflare tunnel;
# x-forwarded-for for ngrok or another proxy that appends the address it saw; remote with no proxy.
# The app's own local-only pages stay local either way (CONSOLE_LOCAL_ONLY).
# CONSOLE_AUTH=local
# CONSOLE_SESSION_HOURS=12
# CONSOLE_SESSION_KEY=
# CONSOLE_LINK_FILE=
# CONSOLE_CLIENT_ADDRESS=auto

# Several apps on one server (guide 14.15): a host.yaml beside the apps (or the file --host names, or
# HOST_FILE in the environment pnpm start runs in, not a settings file) lists them and points the numbers callers dial at each, a call to any other
# number reaching its default. The server's settings, what every app shares (the carrier, the model,
# PUBLIC_HOST, the stores, the numbers host.yaml names), are then in the .env beside host.yaml; this
# app's own .env is read only for the variables its YAML names (its transfer targets, startParameters,
# called numbers), its services' URLs (host.yaml's services), and its own HANDOFF_NUMBER and chat
# sign-in (CHAT_AUDIENCE, CHAT_ISSUER, CHAT_JWKS_URL: its own identity provider), for this app alone.
# Unset, a server runs its one app, as always.
# HOST_FILE=

# Generated wording (guide 16), only for an app whose prompts.yaml or forms.yaml asks for a line to be
# generated (mode: generative, wording: generative): a model words those lines for the moment, each
# checked before anyone hears it, its fixed text said when a check fails or the model is slow. Unset,
# every line is said word for word. RENDER_PROVIDER is openrouter or anthropic; its key is read from
# its own variable (OPENROUTER_API_KEY, ANTHROPIC_API_KEY), never a flag. RENDER_MODEL defaults to a
# current Claude Haiku. RENDER_SHARE is the share of calls served generated (0 to 100, default 100;
# the rest are served fixed, and Insights compares the two). RENDER_TIMEOUT_VOICE_MS and RENDER_TIMEOUT_CHAT_MS are how long a
# turn waits for a line's wording on a call and in a chat (default 900 and 1500), each on its own;
# RENDER_CHECK_TIMEOUT_VOICE_MS and _CHAT_MS for its checks by the decision model (default 500 and 1000).
# RENDER_ANSWER_TIMEOUT_VOICE_MS and _CHAT_MS for a knowledge answer worded for the caller's question (a span
# from: answer, on a topic marked risk: low), its wording and checks together (default 4000 and 6000): past
# the two budgets above the caller first hears the hold line, past this the passage word for word.
# RENDER_TIMEOUT_MS, RENDER_CHECK_TIMEOUT_MS and RENDER_ANSWER_TIMEOUT_MS set both channels at once; a channel's own setting wins. The price, for the cost, is the default model's unless set,
# in dollars a million tokens.
# This app generates one thing only: the short summary of the caller's message inside the read-back
# (prompts.yaml's confirm_take_message). Unset, the read-back says the message word for word. The
# render key may be kept in a file of its own: ENV_FILE=apps/secretary/.env,<your render.env> reads both.
# RENDER_PROVIDER=
# RENDER_MODEL=
# RENDER_SHARE=100
# RENDER_TIMEOUT_VOICE_MS=900
# RENDER_TIMEOUT_CHAT_MS=1500
# RENDER_CHECK_TIMEOUT_VOICE_MS=500
# RENDER_CHECK_TIMEOUT_CHAT_MS=1000
# RENDER_ANSWER_TIMEOUT_VOICE_MS=4000
# RENDER_ANSWER_TIMEOUT_CHAT_MS=6000
# RENDER_PRICE_INPUT_PER_MTOK=
# RENDER_PRICE_OUTPUT_PER_MTOK=
